'pragmat'title="macro pitfall".
$ A rule listed in a 'macro' pragmat is not called: its body is copied
$ into the place of the call, with the formal affixes replaced by the
$ actual ones. This is faster, but may change the meaning.
$ Here 'swap' and 'swap macro' have the same body. Called as
$ swap+x+y+x it exchanges x and y: the out affix gets its value only
$ when the rule returns. Substituted as a macro, the body becomes
$     (y->x, x->y)
$ and both variables end up with the value of y.
$ Shows: the macro pragmat, in/out affixes, why macros need care.

'function'swap+>a+>b>+c>: b->c,a->b.
'function'swap macro+>a+>b>+c>: b->c,a->b.
'pragmat'macro=swap macro.

'root'-x-y:
   1->x,2->y,swap+x+y+x,
   printf+"rule:  x=%d y=%d (exchanged)%n"+x+y,
   1->x,2->y,swap macro+x+y+x,
   printf+"macro: x=%d y=%d (not what we wanted)%n"+x+y.
'end'
